{% from "macros.html.j2" import section, plot, tabs %}
{% call section(step, title) %}
  Every point has a density rho (from the distance to its 5th neighbour) and a delta (distance to
  the nearest denser point). Roots are the points above both elbow cuts,
  rho &ge; <b>{{ forest.rho_cut|fmt('.3f') }}</b> and
  delta &ge; <b>{{ forest.delta_cut|fmt('.3f') }}</b>: dense, and far from anything denser. This
  gives <b>{{ forest.n_roots }}</b> roots.
{% endcall %}
{% set decision %}
<p class="lede">
  Each dot is a hit, coloured by the tree it ends up in. Right means a crowded neighbourhood; up
  means it must travel far to reach anything denser. Most hits sit low: a denser hit is close by,
  so they are on the slope of some hill. Hilltops are both crowded and far from anything denser,
  so they land in the top-right corner, past both cut lines. High dots on the left are loners,
  not centres.
</p>
{{ plot(forest.decision) }}
{% endset %}
{% set separation %}
<p class="lede">
  How clearly each peak stands out. Every root is dense enough; its bar is how far it is from
  anything denser. Tall bars are clear, separate peaks; bars just above the cut are marginal and
  would merge with a slightly stricter cut. Grey bars are the best dense points that did not make
  it.
</p>
{{ plot(forest.separation) }}
{% endset %}
{% set up_close %}
<p class="lede">
  A slice of the real forest: up to {{ forest.up_close.per_tree }} random hits from every cluster,
  plus every hit on their path up to the root, laid out once on real distances, so near means
  similar and positions stay fixed as you add clusters. Each line joins a hit to its parent, the
  nearest denser hit; bigger dots are denser; stars are roots. Pick clusters below to compare
  them; it starts with the two large clusters that touch most.
</p>
<div class="picker" data-trees="{{ forest.n_roots }}">
  <button class="ctl" onclick="pickAll(true)">all</button>
  <button class="ctl" onclick="pickAll(false)">none</button>
  {% for tree in forest.up_close.picks %}
  <button class="pick{{ ' on' if tree.on }}" data-t="{{ tree.id }}" style="--c:{{ tree.color }}" onclick="pick(this)">c{{ tree.id }}</button>
  {% endfor %}
</div>
{{ plot(forest.up_close.figure) }}
{% endset %}
{% set landscape %}
<p class="lede">
  Each tree drawn as a hill: height is density, lines are parent links, the root sits on top. The
  horizontal position is only the order of points along their tree, not a distance; read this
  chart for depth and density. Click a cluster in the legend to isolate it, double-click others
  to add them.
</p>
{{ plot(forest.landscape) }}
{% endset %}
{{ tabs("forest", [
  ("Decision graph", decision),
  ("Peak separation", separation),
  ("Forest up close", up_close),
  ("Forest landscape", landscape),
]) }}
